[gd_scene format=3 uid="uid://sgwr4vku1sa8"]

; A scene built for ONE question: does weight-and-momentum feel right?
;
; Deliberately contains no grapple anchors. An earlier round of this project was
; spent judging one grapple style while actually playing another, and a scene
; with every mechanic in it cannot answer a question about one of them.
;
; The layout is a single line along Z so it is easy to reason about:
;
;    z -65..-55   start shelf, 9.1 m up
;    z -55..-12   GENTLE RAMP, 12 degrees  -> builds speed, but you can stand on it
;    z -12..+12   plaza, y=0               -> enemies, kegs, room to turn around
;    z +12..+24   THE GAP                  -> 12 m, only crossable with ramp speed
;                                              (three crumbling slabs bridge it,
;                                               and your own weight breaks them)
;    z +24..+42   landing shelf            -> the reward, with a checkpoint
;
;    x +15..+36   STEEP RAMP, 30 degrees   -> too steep to stand on, you slide
;    x +36..+48   high shelf, 12 m up      -> slam back down from here
;
; The two ramp angles are chosen against the thresholds that fall out of
; `slope_gravity` (see the Weight group in player.gd): 12 degrees is past the
; point where a slope beats `momentum_friction`, so it builds speed, but short of
; the ~27 degrees where it beats `deceleration`. 30 degrees is past both, so you
; cannot stand still on it at all.

